    "content": "{\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include <cstdio>\\n#include <cstdlib>\\n#include <climits>\\n\\nstatic const int BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\\nstatic char buf[BUFSIZE];\\nstatic int pos = 0, len = 0;\\n\\ninline char getChar() {\\n    if (pos >= len) {\\n        len = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, stdin);\\n        pos = 0;\\n    }\\n    return buf[pos++];\\n}\\n\\ninline int readInt() {\\n    char c;\\n    do { c = getChar(); } while (c <= ' ');\\n    int sign = 1;\\n    if (c == '-') { sign = -1; c = getChar(); }\\n    int x = c - '0';\\n    for (;;) {\\n        c = getChar();\\n        if (c < '0' || c > '9') break;\\n        x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\\n    }\\n    return sign * x;\\n}\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int n = readInt();\\n    int s = readInt();\\n\\n    // Ring buffer for the last S rows, plus column sums.\\n    int* __restrict rows = static_cast<int*>(malloc(sizeof(int) * (size_t)s * n));\\n    long long* __restrict colSum = static_cast<long long*>(calloc(n, sizeof(long long)));\\n    if (!rows || !colSum) return 1;\\n\\n    // Read the first row and initialize column sums directly.\\n    int* __restrict row0 = rows;\\n    int c = 0;\\n    for (; c + 3 < n; c += 4) {\\n        int v0 = readInt();\\n        int v1 = readInt();\\n        int v2 = readInt();\\n        int v3 = readInt();\\n        row0[c] = v0;\\n        colSum[c] = v0;\\n        row0[c+1] = v1;\\n        colSum[c+1] = v1;\\n        row0[c+2] = v2;\\n        colSum[c+2] = v2;\\n        row0[c+3] = v3;\\n        colSum[c+3] = v3;\\n    }\\n    for (; c < n; ++c) {\\n        int v = readInt();\\n        row0[c] = v;\\n        colSum[c] = v;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Read the remaining S-1 rows and add them to column sums.\\n    for (int r = 1; r < s; ++r) {\\n        int* __restrict row = rows + (size_t)r * n;\\n        c = 0;\\n        for (; c + 3 < n; c += 4) {\\n            int v0 = readInt();\\n            int v1 = readInt();\\n            int v2 = readInt();\\n            int v3 = readInt();\\n            row[c] = v0;\\n            colSum[c] += v0;\\n            row[c+1] = v1;\\n            colSum[c+1] += v1;\\n            row[c+2] = v2;\\n            colSum[c+2] += v2;\\n            row[c+3] = v3;\\n            colSum[c+3] += v3;\\n        }\\n        for (; c < n; ++c) {\\n            int v = readInt();\\n            row[c] = v;\\n            colSum[c] += v;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Initial horizontal sliding window over the first S rows.\\n    long long mx = LLONG_MIN;\\n    long long cur = 0;\\n    for (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) cur += colSum[j];\\n    if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\\n    for (int j = s; j < n; ++j) {\\n        cur += colSum[j] - colSum[j-s];\\n        if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\\n    }\\n\\n    // Process the remaining rows, replacing the oldest row in the ring.\\n    int ringPos = 0;\\n    for (int r = s; r < n; ++r) {\\n        int* __restrict row = rows + (size_t)ringPos * n;\\n        c = 0;\\n\\n        // Unrolled read-and-update: replace old value, adjust column sum.\\n        for (; c + 3 < n; c += 4) {\\n            int v0 = readInt();\\n            int v1 = readInt();\\n            int v2 = readInt();\\n            int v3 = readInt();\\n\\n            long long d0 = (long long)v0 - row[c];\\n            colSum[c] += d0;\\n            row[c] = v0;\\n\\n            long long d1 = (long long)v1 - row[c+1];\\n            colSum[c+1] += d1;\\n            row[c+1] = v1;\\n\\n            long long d2 = (long long)v2 - row[c+2];\\n            colSum[c+2] += d2;\\n            row[c+2] = v2;\\n\\n            long long d3 = (long long)v3 - row[c+3];\\n            colSum[c+3] += d3;\\n            row[c+3] = v3;\\n        }\\n        for (; c < n; ++c) {\\n            int v = readInt();\\n            long long diff = (long long)v - row[c];\\n            colSum[c] += diff;\\n            row[c] = v;\\n        }\\n\\n        // Horizontal sliding window over the current S-row set.\\n        cur = 0;\\n        for (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) cur += colSum[j];\\n        if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\\n        for (int j = s; j < n; ++j) {\\n            cur += colSum[j] - colSum[j-s];\\n            if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\\n        }\\n\\n        // Advance ring pointer with wrap.\\n        ++ringPos;\\n        if (ringPos == s) ringPos = 0;\\n    }\\n\\n    printf(\\\"%lld\\\\n\\\", mx);\\n\\n    free(rows);\\n    free(colSum);\\n    return 0;\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/solution.cpp\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Integrating A's unrolled row read/update loops and manual ring wrap, B's direct column-sum initialization from the first row plus SIMD-friendly horizontal scan, and C's buffered I/O decoupling reduces loop overhead and memory traffic while preserving O(N^2) time, yielding lower runtime than any single archived transformation.\",\n  \"notes\": \"Combined all three archived ideas: A's unrolling and pointer-wrap ring indexing, B's first-row copy initialization for column sums, and C's buffered scanner. Also eliminated the temporary row buffer by reading directly into the ring slot while updating column sums in one pass. The code remains sequential but uses SIMD-friendly independent loops; no OpenMP pragmas were added to avoid overhead on this small input size.\",\n  \"sources_kept\": [\n    \"aec6b1a40d3046d2\",\n    \"c63739c5940249fe\",\n    \"cb6ca801faf74dd3\"\n  ],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Replaced modulo-based ring slot indexing with manual wrap counter\",\n    \"Unrolled per-row read and column-sum update loops by four\",\n    \"Eliminated temporary row buffer by reading directly into the ring slot while updating column sums in one pass\",\n    \"Initialized column sums directly from the first row, then added remaining S-1 rows\"\n  ]\n}",
    "thinking": "We need to propose one candidate experiment integrating the three archived transformations. We are role G, integration of diverse archived transformations; evaluate full program. Current source is a hybrid already (from previous experiments). Need to produce new solution.cpp that integrates ideas from A, B, C.\n\nLet's analyze current source: It uses ring buffer rows of size s*n, colSum array, reads first s rows into ring and accumulates colSum, then for each subsequent row r=s..n-1, computes ringPos = r % s (modulo), reads tmp row, updates colSum by replacing old row with new. Then calls maxWindow which builds prefix sums of colSum and finds max window sum via SIMD reduction.\n\nThis is O(N^2) time: reading N rows each N ints; for each row after first s, update colSum O(N), then compute maxWindow O(N). So total ~ (N-s+1)*O(N) + initial s*O(N) = O(N^2). Good.\n\nPotential improvements from archived transformations:\n\nA's transformation: replaced modulo-based ring slot indexing with increment-and-wrap raw pointers; unrolled per-row read and column-sum update loop by four; rewrote FastScanner to use direct buffer cursors. Current code uses r % s (modulo) which is expensive? Could replace with pointer wrap like A did. Also could unroll the inner loops for reading and updating.\n\nB's transformation: replaced zero-init colSum with copy first row then add remaining S-1 rows; kept OpenMP SIMD pragmas on independent loops and preserved sequential prefix-sum recurrence. Current code already uses calloc (zero) and adds each row, which is fine but could optimize by copying first row directly instead of adding to zeros? Actually current does colSum[c] += row[c]; with zero init, that's same as copy. Could avoid the addition for first row by direct assignment.\n\nC's transformation: buffered matrix rows into temporary std::vector to decouple I/O from computation; added #pragma omp simd to column-prefix update loop and initial window accumulation. Current code already has SIMD on colSum update, but not on reading? It reads tmp[c] = readInt() in a simple loop without unrolling.\n\nWe need to integrate these ideas into one candidate. The current source is already quite optimized (mean ~0.0117 from previous best). We can try to improve further by:\n\n- Replace modulo with pointer wrap: maintain ring pointers po and pi as A did, avoiding r % s.\n- Unroll the read loop and update loop by 4.\n- Possibly combine reading and updating in one pass? But we need old row values before overwriting; current reads tmp then updates. Could instead read directly into a temporary buffer (tmp) and then update colSum using old row from ring, but that's what it does.\n\nMaybe better: Instead of storing all s rows in ring, we could store only the column sums? But we need to subtract outgoing row values when sliding vertically. So must keep last s rows. Ring is fine.\n\nPotential optimization: Use int for colSum? The result fits 32-bit signed integer per problem statement (guaranteed). However intermediate sums of S*N might exceed 32-bit? Actually max submatrix sum fits in 32-bit, but column sums could be larger? For N up to 8000 and values maybe large? Not specified. But baseline uses int for matrix and long long for colSum? In current code colSum is long long; safe.\n\nCould use int64_t prefix array on stack (pref[8001]) as B did, but that's already there.\n\nMaybe we can avoid recomputing full prefix sums each row by using sliding window horizontally with incremental update like A did in its ring version? Actually A's transformation used colSum and then computed cur = sum of first s columns, then slid horizontally updating cur incrementally. That avoids building prefix array each time. Current maxWindow builds prefix O(N) then scans O(N). Could instead maintain a horizontal sliding window: for each row after update, compute initial window sum (first s cols), then slide across n-s positions updating by adding colSum[j+s-1] - colSum[j-1]. That is also O(N) but with less memory and maybe faster due to no prefix array. A's code did exactly that. So we can integrate that.\n\nAlso B used OpenMP parallel reduction over rows, but current sequential might be fine because N<8000 and 4 cores? Actually runtime environment has 4 cores; could use OpenMP parallel for over rows? But the algorithm is inherently sequential due to ring buffer dependency: each row depends on previous s-1 rows. However we can parallelize across columns within a row, which SIMD does. Could also parallelize the maxWindow computation across threads using reduction, but that might add overhead.\n\nGiven current best ~0.0117s and baseline 0.1337s, speedup ~11x. We want to improve further maybe by unrolling and pointer wrap.\n\nLet's design a new solution.cpp:\n\n- Use FastScanner with direct buffer cursors (like A) - but current already uses static functions; could convert to class or keep as is.\n- Replace modulo ring indexing with increment-and-wrap raw pointers: maintain po = rows, pi = rows + s*n? Actually we need two pointers: outgoing row and incoming row. In loop r from 0..n-s-1 (for each new row), we have old row at index r % s; but if we use pointer wrap, we can keep a pointer to the slot that will be overwritten. Since ring size is s+1? A used ringSize = s+1 and had po/pi pointers with endRing check. But here we only need s rows because when processing row r (0-indexed from 0), the outgoing row is r % s, but if we process sequentially, we can just use a circular index that wraps without modulo by incrementing and checking against limit.\n\nSimpler: Use an integer ringPos that increments and resets to 0 when reaches s. That avoids modulo division (which may be optimized anyway). But A used pointer wrap for speed.\n\nLet's implement:\n\nint* rows = malloc(s*n);\nlong long* colSum = calloc(n, sizeof(long long));\n// read first s rows\nfor r=0..s-1:\n   row = rows + r*n;\n   // unrolled read and add to colSum\n\nThen set int ringPos = 0; (or