ith unrolling? Let's look at A's code:\n\nfor (; j + 3 < n; j += 4) {\n    int v0 = fs.nextInt();\n    ...\n    pi[j] = v0;\n    c[j] += (long long)v0 - po[j];\n    ...\n}\n\nSo it reads four ints, stores them into ring slot and updates colSum in same iteration. That's fine.\n\nWe can do similar: read 4 ints at a time, update row[c..c+3] and colSum accordingly.\n\nAlso we need to handle the initial s rows similarly with unrolling.\n\nLet's design:\n\n- Use FastScanner class or static functions; current is okay but maybe convert to direct buffer cursors as A did (cur/end pointers) for speed. Current uses pos/len indices, which may be slightly slower due to bounds check each call? Actually getChar checks if pos>=len then refill; that's fine.\n\n- Use malloc instead of new[].\n\nLet's write code:\n\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <climits>\n\nstatic const int BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\nstatic char buf[BUFSIZE];\nstatic int pos = 0, len = 0;\n\ninline char getChar() {\n    if (pos >= len) {\n        len = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, stdin);\n        pos = 0;\n    }\n    return buf[pos++];\n}\n\ninline int readInt() {\n    char c;\n    do { c = getChar(); } while (c <= ' ');\n    int sign = 1;\n    if (c == '-') { sign = -1; c = getChar(); }\n    int x = c - '0';\n    for (;;) {\n        c = getChar();\n        if (c < '0' || c > '9') break;\n        x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\n    }\n    return sign * x;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n = readInt();\n    int s = readInt();\n\n    // Allocate ring buffer for last s rows\n    int* rows = static_cast<int*>(malloc(sizeof(int) * (size_t)s * n));\n    long long* colSum = static_cast<long long*>(calloc(n, sizeof(long long)));\n    if (!rows || !colSum) return 1;\n\n    // Read first s rows and accumulate column sums\n    for (int r = 0; r < s; ++r) {\n        int* row = rows + (size_t)r * n;\n        int c = 0;\n        // Unroll by 4\n        for (; c + 3 < n; c += 4) {\n            int v0 = readInt();\n            int v1 = readInt();\n            int v2 = readInt();\n            int v3 = readInt();\n            row[c] = v0;\n            colSum[c] += v0;\n            row[c+1] = v1;\n            colSum[c+1] += v1;\n            row[c+2] = v2;\n            colSum[c+2] += v2;\n            row[c+3] = v3;\n            colSum[c+3] += v3;\n        }\n        for (; c < n; ++c) {\n            int v = readInt();\n            row[c] = v;\n            colSum[c] += v;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Compute initial max window sum from first s rows\n    long long mx = LLONG_MIN;\n    long long cur = 0;\n    for (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) cur += colSum[j];\n    if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n    for (int j = s; j < n; ++j) {\n        cur += colSum[j] - colSum[j-s];\n        if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n    }\n\n    // Process remaining rows\n    int ringPos = 0; // will wrap manually\n    for (int r = s; r < n; ++r) {\n        int* row = rows + (size_t)ringPos * n;\n        int c = 0;\n        // Unrolled read and update: replace old value with new, adjust colSum\n        for (; c + 3 < n; c += 4) {\n            int v0 = readInt();\n            int v1 = readInt();\n            int v2 = readInt();\n            int v3 = readInt();\n            long long d0 = (long long)v0 - row[c];\n            colSum[c] += d0;\n            row[c] = v0;\n            long long d1 = (long long)v1 - row[c+1];\n            colSum[c+1] += d1;\n            row[c+1] = v1;\n            long long d2 = (long long)v2 - row[c+2];\n            colSum[c+2] += d2;\n            row[c+2] = v2;\n            long long d3 = (long long)v3 - row[c+3];\n            colSum[c+3] += d3;\n            row[c+3] = v3;\n        }\n        for (; c < n; ++c) {\n            int v = readInt();\n            long long diff = (long long)v - row[c];\n            colSum[c] += diff;\n            row[c] = v;\n        }\n\n        // Compute max window sum for this set of rows\n        cur = 0;\n        for (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) cur += colSum[j];\n        if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n        for (int j = s; j < n; ++j) {\n            cur += colSum[j] - colSum[j-s];\n            if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n        }\n\n        // Advance ring pointer with wrap\n        ++ringPos;\n        if (ringPos == s) ringPos = 0;\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%lld\\n\", mx);\n\n    free(rows);\n    free(colSum);\n    return 0;\n}\n\nThis integrates:\n- A's unrolled read/update loop and manual ring wrap instead of modulo.\n- B's idea? Actually B used copy first row then add; we already do that effectively (colSum starts zero, adding each row). Could optimize by copying first row directly: for r=0, set colSum[c] = v; but current adds to zero which is same. Not a big deal.\n- C's SIMD? We didn't use #pragma omp simd on the update loops because they have function calls (readInt) and dependencies? Actually the loop body has independent operations per column, so we could add #pragma omp simd but readInt() not vectorizable. However after reading into local variables v0..v3, the arithmetic is independent; compiler might auto-vectorize if it can see no aliasing between row and colSum (they are different arrays). We could add __restrict qualifiers to help.\n\nWe should also consider using OpenMP parallel for over rows? But ring buffer dependency prevents that. However we could use a different algorithm: compute column sums for all possible vertical windows via prefix sums of columns, then each horizontal window is independent and can be parallelized across rows. That's what B did with full matrix storage and OpenMP parallel reduction over row starts. That might be faster if memory bandwidth allows storing entire N*N ints (up to 8000^2 = 64M ints ~256MB, which may fit? But runtime environment maybe limited). Current ring buffer uses only s*n memory, much less.\n\nGiven current best is already good, we can try this integrated version. It should be correct and likely faster due to unrolling and avoiding modulo.\n\nWe need to ensure correctness: The algorithm computes max sum of SxS submatrix by sliding vertical window (rows) and for each set of s consecutive rows, compute horizontal max via sliding window on column sums. That's standard O(N^2). Our implementation does that.\n\nCheck edge cases: n up to 7999? Actually N<8000, so n <= 7999. s < n, s>0. The ring buffer size s*n fits in memory (max ~64M ints = 256MB) but we only allocate s rows; if s close to n, that's almost full matrix anyway. But okay.\n\nPotential issue: In the initial reading of first s rows, we use colSum[c] += v; with calloc zero init, fine. For subsequent rows, we update by replacing old row value. The ringPos cycles through 0..s-1 in order, so when r=s, ringPos=0 overwrites row 0 (which is the oldest), correct.\n\nWe should also consider using long long for cur and mx; result fits int but intermediate sums may exceed? Actually max submatrix sum fits 32-bit signed, but column sums could be larger than that if values are large? For example N=8000, each value up to maybe 10^9? Then colSum of s rows could be up to 8e12 which exceeds 32-bit. So long long is necessary for intermediate computations. Our code uses long long.\n\nNow we need to produce JSON with files content and metadata.\n\nWe must include sources_kept: integration_source_ids are [\"aec6b1a40d3046d2\", \"c63739c5940249fe\", \"cb6ca801faf74dd3\"]. We should keep all three since we integrated ideas from each. So sources_kept = those IDs.\n\nTransformations list: describe what we did, e.g., \"Unrolled row read and column-sum update loops by four\", \"Replaced modulo ring indexing with manual wrap counter\", \"Eliminated temporary row buffer by reading directly into ring slot while updating column sums\", \"Used sliding horizontal window on column sums instead of prefix array\".\n\nHypothesis: something like integrating A's unrolling/pointer-wrap, B's direct initialization and SIMD-friendly scan, C's buffered I/O decoupling yields lower runtime.\n\nNotes: mention we kept all three source ideas.\n\nNow ensure the code compiles with g++ -O3 -fopenmp? We didn't use OpenMP pragmas; but maybe compiler flags include -fopenmp. Our code doesn't need it, but if Makefile uses -fopenmp, fine. Could add #pragma omp simd to the horizontal window loops? They are sequential dependencies (cur depends on previous), so not vectorizable easily. But we could use reduction over j with prefix sums as before; however sliding window is inherently sequential. The current code's maxWindow used SIMD reduction over independent windows via prefix array, which might be faster than sequential sliding if compiler can vectorize the reduction. Actually the sliding loop has a dependency on cur, so cannot vectorize. But we could compute all window sums independently using prefix sums and then reduce with SIMD, as B did. That may be faster because it allows vectorization across j. Let's compare: For each row set, computing prefix array O(N) then reduction over N-s+1 windows with SIMD might be similar to sliding loop but can use SIMD. However building prefix is also sequential (pref[i+1]=pref[i]+col[i]) which has dependency; but that's a simple addition chain that compiler may vectorize? Not easily due to dependency. Actually prefix sum can be parallelized with scan algorithms, but not trivial.\n\nGiven current best used maxWindow with prefix and SIMD reduction, maybe we should keep that approach instead of sliding window. But A's code used sliding window without prefix; it was fast (0.0113). Our previous experiments showed a8a1165d375b4a5a had 0.01172 using ring buffer + maxWindow? Actually the current source is from some parent, not sure which. The selected_parent is 1d7d1c3849e54962 with mean 0.01225; but there was a8a1165d375b4a5a with 0.01172 (best among shown). That one likely used ring buffer + maxWindow? Let's check: In interactions, the third entry had sources [\"aec6b1a40d3046d2\", \"a5850332c2144560\", \"2d8da312c5194b7e\"] and mean 0.01172; but those source IDs are not in our integration list? Actually integration_source_ids are only three: aec6..., c637..., cb6c... So we don't have the others.\n\nAnyway, we need to propose one candidate. We can choose either sliding window or prefix+SIMD reduction. Since A's transformation used sliding window and was fast (0.0113), maybe that is better. But our current code uses maxWindow with prefix; we could switch to sliding window as in A. Let's do that.\n\nAlso, we should consider using __restrict on pointers to help compiler vectorize the update loops. Add `int* __restrict row` and `long long* __restrict colSum`.\n\nLet's refine code:\n\n- Use static inline functions for reading.\n- In main, use malloc/calloc.\n- For initial s rows: unrolled read and add.\n- Compute mx via sliding window (as above).\n- For each subsequent row: unrolled read/update, then compute cur via sliding.\n\nWe can also combine the horizontal scan into a helper function to avoid code duplication. But inline might be better for performance.\n\nLet's write final code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <climits>\n\nstatic const int BUFSIZE = 1 << 20;\nstatic char buf[BUFSIZE];\nstatic int pos = 0, len = 0;\n\ninline char getChar() {\n    if (pos >= len) {\n        len = fread(buf, 1, BUFSIZE, stdin);\n        pos = 0;\n    }\n    return buf[pos++];\n}\n\ninline int readInt() {\n    char c;\n    do { c = getChar(); } while (c <= ' ');\n    int sign = 1;\n    if (c == '-') { sign = -1; c = getChar(); }\n    int x = c - '0';\n    for (;;) {\n        c = getChar();\n        if (c < '0' || c > '9') break;\n        x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\n    }\n    return sign * x;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n = readInt();\n    int s = readInt();\n\n    // Allocate ring buffer for last s rows and column sums\n    int* __restrict rows = static_cast<int*>(malloc(sizeof(int) * (size_t)s * n));\n    long long* __restrict colSum = static_cast<long long*>(calloc(n, sizeof(long long)));\n    if (!rows || !colSum) return 1;\n\n    // Read first s rows and accumulate column sums\n    for (int r = 0; r < s; ++r) {\n        int* __restrict row = rows + (size_t)r * n;\n        int c = 0;\n        // Unroll by 4\n        for (; c + 3 < n; c += 4) {\n            int v0 = readInt();\n            int v1 = readInt();\n            int v2 = readInt();\n            int v3 = readInt();\n            row[c] = v0;\n            colSum[c] += v0;\n            row[c+1] = v1;\n            colSum[c+1] += v1;\n            row[c+2] = v2;\n            colSum[c+2] += v2;\n            row[c+3] = v3;\n            colSum[c+3] += v3;\n        }\n        for (; c < n; ++c) {\n            int v = readInt();\n            row[c] = v;\n            colSum[c] += v;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Compute initial max window sum from first s rows\n    long long mx = LLONG_MIN;\n    long long cur = 0;\n    for (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) cur += colSum[j];\n    if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n    for (int j = s; j < n; ++j) {\n        cur += colSum[j] - colSum[j-s];\n        if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n    }\n\n    // Process remaining rows\n    int ringPos = 0;\n    for (int r = s; r < n; ++r) {\n        int* __restrict row = rows + (size_t)ringPos * n;\n        int c = 0;\n        // Unrolled read and update: replace old value with new, adjust colSum\n        for (; c + 3 < n; c += 4) {\n            int v0 = readInt();\n            int v1 = readInt();\n            int v2 = readInt();\n            int v3 = readInt();\n            long long d0 = (long long)v0 - row[c];\n            colSum[c] += d0;\n            row[c] = v0;\n            long long d1 = (long long)v1 - row[c+1];\n            colSum[c+1] += d1;\n            row[c+1] = v1;\n            long long d2 = (long long)v2 - row[c+2];\n            colSum[c+2] += d2;\n            row[c+2] = v2;\n            long long d3 = (long long)v3 - row[c+3];\n            colSum[c+3] += d3;\n            row[c+3] = v3;\n        }\n        for (; c < n; ++c) {\n            int v = readInt();\n            long long diff = (long long)v - row[c];\n            colSum[c] += diff;\n            row[c] = v;\n        }\n\n        // Compute max window sum for this set of rows\n        cur = 0;\n        for (int j = 0; j < s; ++j) cur += colSum[j];\n        if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n        for (int j = s; j < n; ++j) {\n            cur += colSum[j] - colSum[j-s];\n            if (cur > mx) mx = cur;\n        }\n\n        // Advance ring pointer with wrap\n        ++ringPos;\n        if (ringPos == s) ringPos = 0;\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%lld\\n\", mx);\n\n    free(rows);\n    free(colSum);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nThis should be correct. Let's double-check logic: For each new row r from s to n-1, we overwrite the oldest row (ringPos cycles). The colSum initially contains sum of rows 0..s-1. After processing row s, ringPos=0 overwrites row 0 with row s; colSum becomes sum of rows 1..s. That's correct for vertical window starting at row 1. Then we compute horizontal max over that set. Good.\n\nEdge case: n=s? But problem says S<N so s<n always. So there is at least one new row after initial s.\n\nNow, should we use `long long` for cur and mx; yes.\n\nPotential performance: The unrolled loops read 4 ints sequentially from stdin; that's fine. The update arithmetic uses long long differences; could be optimized by compiler.\n\nWe might also add `#pragma omp simd` to the horizontal scan? Not possible due to dependency on cur. But we could use prefix array and SIMD reduction as before, but sliding is simpler and likely fast enough given A's performance.\n\nLet's consider if there is any risk of integer overflow in colSum: values are ints (maybe up to 10^9), s up to 7999, so sum up to ~8e12 fits long long. Good.\n\nNow produce JSON response with files array containing this content for src/solution.cpp.\n\nWe need to include transformations list and hypothesis.\n\nLet's craft:\n\nhypothesis: \"Integrating A's unrolled row read/update loops and manual ring wrap, B's direct column-sum initialization (via first-